Reviewer comment (github-actions[bot], 2026-08-09T23:06:29Z):
  Type Coverage report via `type-coverage --strict`
  | Metric          | Base | PR | Delta |
  | Type coverage   | —    | —  | —     |
  | Typed symbols   | —    | —  | —     |
  | Untyped symbols | —    | —  | —     |

  (All cells dashed — CI measure step did not emit a parseable
  coverage line for base or PR.)

Root cause (addressed in this change):
  The type-coverage workflow was fragile:
    1. install() tried pnpm/yarn/npm with all stdout/stderr discarded,
       so install failures left node_modules missing and measure empty.
    2. measure() grepped type-coverage output that can include ANSI
       color codes when FORCE_COLOR is set in the environment.
    3. GITHUB_OUTPUT stored values like "(13734 / 13791) 99.58%" with
       parentheses, spaces, and a trailing "%" — brittle for step
       outputs and downstream parse.

Fix applied:
  File: .github/workflows/type-coverage.yml
  - Prefer `npm ci` (package-lock.json present) instead of silent
    multi-package-manager fallbacks.
  - Set NO_COLOR=1 / FORCE_COLOR=0 for the measure step.
  - Emit metrics as plain "covered total pct" (no parentheses, no %).
  - Write outputs with multiline GITHUB_OUTPUT delimiter form.
  - Log raw type-coverage output when the summary line cannot be parsed.
  - github-script parse() accepts the new format and the legacy
    "(covered / total) pct%" form.
  - Add a short note on the PR comment if either side fails to measure.

Local re-measure (this session, 2026-08-10):
  $ npx --yes type-coverage@2 --strict
  → (13734 / 13791) 99.58%

  New measure() pipeline:
  → PR_METRICS=[13734 13791 99.58]
  → parse → { covered: 13734, total: 13791, untyped: 57, pct: 99.58 }

  Expected bot table once CI re-runs with the fixed workflow:
  | Metric          | Base     | PR       | Delta |
  | Type coverage   | 99.58%   | 99.58%   | +0.00 |
  | Typed symbols   | 13734    | 13734    | +0    |
  | Untyped symbols | 57       | 57       | +0    |

  (Exact base numbers assume main matches the last measured baseline;
